  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "At the bar, Nagiyev proudly shows Vika and Kostya page fourteen of his passport: he is officially divorced from Kristina and did not lose his restaurant in the settlement.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я развёлся!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Кристина Семёновна, до свидания!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Четырнадцатая страница!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "В лесах среди медведей.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Так, пойду организую шампанское для гостей.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давай, беги, маленький кролик.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Дмитрий Владимирович, я вас поздравляю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, сынок.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мне очень нравится, как ты работаешь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Молодец, красавчик.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Спасибо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хочу тебя поблагодарить.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я развёлся!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I got divorced!' An emphatic spoken line centered on the verb meaning 'got divorced'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Кристина Семёновна, до свидания!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Kristina Semyonovna, goodbye!' Dmitry Nagiyev anchors the line on 'Kristina', while 'goodbye' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Четырнадцатая страница!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Page fourteen!' The grammar packs 'fourteenth' and 'page' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "В лесах среди медведей.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In the woods among the bears.' The meaning hinges on 'woods' working together with 'bears' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Так, пойду организую шампанское для гостей.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Right, I will go arrange champagne for the guests.' In Russian, the idea of 'so' flows naturally into 'guests' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давай, беги, маленький кролик.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Go on, run, little rabbit.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'come on' and finishing on 'rabbit'. Kostya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Дмитрий Владимирович, я вас поздравляю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Dmitry Vladimirovich, I congratulate you.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'I' before landing on 'congratulations'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, сынок.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, my boy.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'yes' before landing on 'sonny'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мне очень нравится, как ты работаешь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I really like how you work.' The grammar packs 'to me' and 'are working' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Молодец, красавчик.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well done, champ.' Dmitry Nagiyev anchors the line on 'well done', while 'good-looking guy' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Спасибо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Thank you.' The grammar packs 'thank you' and 'thank you' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хочу тебя поблагодарить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I want to thank you.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'want' and finishing on 'to thank'. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
